course: everyday
lesson: 398
title: Sore losers and button-mashers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Theo lost three races in a row and threw the controller onto the sofa. It bounced onto the floor, the battery cover snapped off, and Theo had to tell his mum. Anger takes one second; a broken controller and a cross mum last much longer. Next time Theo felt the heat rising, he put the controller down and walked to the kitchen for a glass of water first.
explain: Noah had practised fighting-game combos for weeks. Ruby had never played; she just pressed every button as fast as she could, and she won. Noah said it was the most unfair thing in history. Then he did something clever: he taught Ruby two real moves. Soon she was beating him on purpose instead of by accident. Button-mashing can win a round; practice wins a season.
explain: Every losing style was on show that month. Kai said «the controller is broken» every time he lost, so Mia swapped controllers with him; he lost again and everyone, even Kai, laughed. Ollie said «I let you win» every single time, until Zara said «then let me win again», and Ollie admitted he had tried his hardest. Noah quit matches just before he lost so they would not count, until the group made a rule: quitting counts as a loss. Leo sulked and refused pizza, and by the time he wanted some there was one slice left, with no cheese.
explain: Winners could be just as bad. Priya won and did a long victory dance right in front of the TV. Next time she lost, Leo did the exact same dance, and she understood. Mia always won at her own house because she played every day, so she started picking games nobody had played, and everyone began equal.
explain: Jayden cried after losing a football game final on penalties and felt babyish. Then Theo said he had cried when his real team lost the cup, and Jayden felt less alone. And Ruby, beaten by a gloating boy, simply said «good game». He did not know what to say. The next week he said «good game» first.
fact: Anger at a game comes fast, but broken things and broken trust last longer.
fact: Putting the controller down and walking away for a minute helps the hot feeling pass.
fact: Button-mashing can win once, but practice wins more often over time.
fact: An excuse used every time, like «the controller is broken», turns into a joke about you.
fact: Saying «I let you win» does not hide a loss; it only spoils the other person's win.
fact: Quitting a match to avoid a loss is unfair to the other players.
fact: A long victory dance in front of the loser rubs it in; a short celebration and «good game» is better.
fact: Playing every day gives you a big head start, so a fair host evens it out.
fact: Strong feelings about games are normal, even for grown-ups.
fact: Sulking usually takes away more than the loss did.
fact: Good sportsmanship spreads from one player to another.
exercise: What happened after Theo threw the controller? | The battery cover snapped and he had to tell his mum | The controller bounced off the wall and he still won the game | Nobody noticed and they kept playing | Mia gave him a new controller as a gift
exercise: What could Theo do when he feels the heat rising? | Put the controller down and walk away for a minute | Throw it onto the sofa, because sofas are soft so it's fine | Shout at the screen until he feels better | Press the buttons harder to win faster
exercise: Why did Ruby beat Noah the first time? | She button-mashed and got lucky | She had practised in secret for weeks | Noah let her win on purpose | Her controller was the better one
exercise: How did Noah feel when Ruby beat him? | That it was completely unfair | Proud of how fast she had learned | Happy because he wanted to lose | Glad she had practised so much
exercise: What clever thing did Noah do next? | Taught Ruby two real moves | Refused to ever play her again | Told everyone Ruby had cheated | Hid the fighting game from her
exercise: Kai said «the controller is broken» every time he lost. What happened? | They swapped, he lost again and everyone laughed | They bought him a brand new controller | He started winning every single game | Mia's mum fixed the controller
exercise: Why does «I let you win» spoil things? | It takes the win away from the winner | It makes the game crash and restart | It is always true, so it is boring to hear | It makes the loser feel even better
exercise: How did Zara deal with Ollie's «I let you win»? | She said «then let me win again» | She cried and went home | She agreed she had not really won | She told a teacher about it
exercise: Why did quitting before losing feel unfair to the others? | It stole the win they had earned | It made the game far too short | It broke the console's memory | It gave them extra turns
exercise: How did Priya learn that her victory dance rubbed it in? | Leo did the same dance when she lost | Her mum told her dancing is rude | The TV switched off in the middle | She tripped over the controller cable
exercise: Why did Mia always win at her house? | She played every day and had a head start | Her console was secretly set to let her win | The others were not allowed to try | She had the only good controller
exercise: How did Mia make games fair for her guests? | She picked games none of them had played | She let her guests win on purpose every time | She stopped playing at all | She only played with one hand
exercise: How did Jayden feel after crying over the penalties? | Babyish, until Theo said he cried too | Proud of himself and ready to play again | Angry at Theo for laughing at him | Happy because he had won
exercise: What did Leo's sulk cost him? | The good pizza, leaving one cheeseless slice | His turn on the console for the rest of the night | His friendship with Mia | Nothing, sulking always pays off in the end
exercise: What happened after Ruby said «good game» to the gloating boy? | Next week he said «good game» first | He laughed and gloated even more | He never played with her again | He asked her to dance in front of the TV
exercise: Which is a sign of winning well? | A short celebration and «good game» | A long dance in front of the loser | Telling the loser they are rubbish | Saying the loser's controller was broken
test: «this controller's busted, that's why» — 5th time today. What happens to an excuse like that? | It turns into a joke | Everyone starts to believe it after a while | The controller really does break in the end | You get a free extra go
test: Ruby just mashed buttons and wiped the floor with Noah. «that's cheating!!» Is it? | No, it's luck; practice wins more over time | Yes, mashing buttons is against the law | Yes, newbies aren't allowed to win | No, mashing beats practice every time
test: «I LET you win, obviously». The other kid knows he tried his hardest. What's true? | It spoils the win and fools nobody | It's a kind thing to say to a winner | It means the loser is a better player | It means they should play again
test: Noah rage-quits one hit before losing. «doesn't count lol». Best house rule? | Quitting counts as a loss | Quitting lets you try again | The winner has to play again | Whoever quits gets the next turn
test: «nah not hungry» — Leo's sulking about losing, pizza's here. What usually happens? | He misses the good pizza | Everyone saves him the best slices | The loss gets undone | He wins the next game for sure
test: Priya: «WHO'S THE BEST? ME! ME!» dancing in front of the telly for two minutes. What's the problem? | It rubs it in for the losers | Dancing breaks the console | She is blocking the fan | Winners mustn't smile at all
test: Theo's red in the face, fist tight round the controller after 3 losses. Wisest thing? | Put it down and walk off for a minute | Throw it, the sofa's soft so it won't break | Play faster to win it back | Yell at whoever won
test: «stop crying, it's only a game, baby». Jayden lost a final on penalties. What's kinder and truer? | Strong feelings about games are normal | Only babies cry over a game, big kids don't | Crying means you're bad at games | Grown-ups never get upset at games
test: Mia beats everyone at her own house every week. Guests start leaving early. What's going on? | Her head start makes it no real contest | Her guests are just bad at games | Her console is set up to cheat for her | Her guests don't like her house
test: «gg» says Ruby to the kid who just gloated at her. Why bother? | Good manners spread | It makes the gloater feel even better about winning | It means she secretly wants a rematch | It's the rule in every game ever made
test: Teacher: «What wins more matches over a whole season, luck or practice?» | Practice | Luck | Button-mashing | The best controller
exam: Identify the principal consequence, as established in this lesson, of hurling a controller in anger. | Damage to property and to the trust of others, outlasting the anger | The immediate restoration of the player's composure | A guaranteed improvement in subsequent performance | No consequence of any significance whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion «I let you win» uttered by a defeated participant? | It diminishes the victor's achievement without concealing the defeat | It is a gracious acknowledgement of the victor's superior skill | It invariably reflects the true state of affairs | It obliges the victor to offer a rematch
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning success achieved by random pressing of buttons. | It may prevail occasionally, whereas practice prevails more often over time | It prevails more often than practised skill in all cases | It constitutes a breach of the rules of the game | It cannot prevail against any opponent whatsoever
exam: It is given that a participant habitually abandons matches immediately prior to defeat. State the remedy adopted by the group. | That abandonment of a match shall be counted as a loss | That the participant shall be awarded an additional turn | That all matches shall be declared void | That the participant shall be excluded permanently
